#!/usr/bin/env php
<?php
/**
 * Loghound — bring a live index's schema up to date after an upgrade.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * WHAT GOES WRONG WITHOUT THIS
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * A release adds a field. You upgrade the code. Your two indexes are still running the
 * configset that was uploaded when you installed, and the only dynamic field either schema
 * declares is `*` mapped to the `ignored` type. So the tailer writes the new field, Solr
 * accepts the document, the value is discarded, and NOTHING REPORTS AN ERROR — not the
 * daemons, not the platform, not the panel. Months later a facet is empty and there is no
 * trace of why, and the values that were dropped are not recoverable.
 *
 * Run this after every upgrade. It is one read per index and it changes nothing without
 * --apply.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * USAGE
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *   loghound-schema                 compare, change nothing (the default)
 *   loghound-schema --check         the same, spelled out
 *   loghound-schema --apply         upload this release's configsets where they are missing
 *                                   fields, then re-check
 *   loghound-schema --quiet         print only what is wrong, and the verdict
 *   loghound-schema --help
 *
 * EXIT CODES, which is the half a deployment script reads:
 *
 *   0  up to date — every field this release writes is declared by both live schemas
 *      (and, after --apply, the push succeeded)
 *   1  this installation is not usable: bad flags, unusable configuration, no indexes yet
 *   2  failed — a schema could not be read, or an upload was rejected
 *   3  out of date — at least one index is missing at least one field (--check only;
 *      --apply never leaves this state, it either fixes it or exits 2)
 *   4  needs migrating — an index still declares Solr's MANAGED schema factory, so it owns the
 *      schema file and every schema upload to that index is a silent no-op. Distinct from 2 on
 *      purpose: 2 means the check could not be made and a retry may answer, 4 means the check
 *      was made, the answer is known, and nothing changes until --apply switches the factory.
 *
 *   if loghound-schema --check; then deploy; else echo "run --apply first"; fi
 *
 * The verdict is also written to `var/schema-check.json` — the same file the panel's Settings
 * page reads, so running this on the shell updates what the browser shows.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * WHAT IT WILL NOT DO
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * It never pushes to an index whose live schema it could not read. A push replaces the whole
 * configset, so pushing blind to an index that might be running a NEWER schema would delete
 * the fields that newer release writes — the same silent data loss this tool exists to stop,
 * caused by the tool. It refuses and says so instead; re-run it when the platform answers.
 *
 * An extra field — one the index declares and this release does not write — is not an error
 * and is never "fixed". That is a newer schema, or a field a later release dropped, and it
 * costs nothing.
 *
 * Refuses to run under a web SAPI. --apply reconfigures and reloads a live index, and an
 * unauthenticated HTTP route to that is not something to leave lying around because of where
 * somebody copied this file to.
 *
 * The Opensolr API key is read from config/loghound.php and is never printed, not by this
 * script and not by anything it calls: every message from the control plane goes through
 * Opensolr::redact() before it can reach a terminal.
 *
 * @package Loghound
 * @license MIT
 */

declare(strict_types=1);

if (PHP_SAPI !== 'cli') {
    http_response_code(404);
    exit;
}

require __DIR__ . '/../src/autoload.php';

use Loghound\Config;
use Loghound\Setup\Schema;

const LH_SCHEMA_OK        = Schema::EXIT_OK;
const LH_SCHEMA_CONFIG    = Schema::EXIT_CONFIG;
const LH_SCHEMA_FAILED    = Schema::EXIT_FAILED;
const LH_SCHEMA_OUTDATED  = Schema::EXIT_OUTDATED;
const LH_SCHEMA_MANAGED   = Schema::EXIT_MANAGED;

$root       = dirname(__DIR__);
$configPath = getenv('LOGHOUND_CONFIG') ?: $root . '/config/loghound.php';

$apply = false;
$quiet = false;
$force = false;

foreach (array_slice($argv, 1) as $arg) {
    switch ($arg) {
        case '--apply':
            $apply = true;
            break;
        /* FORCE IMPLIES APPLY. Asking for --force alone and being told nothing happened, because
           the flag that does the uploading was also required, is a trap with no upside. */
        case '--force':
            $apply = true;
            $force = true;
            break;
        case '--check':
            break;
        case '--quiet':
            $quiet = true;
            break;
        case '--help':
        case '-h':
            lh_schema_usage();
            exit(LH_SCHEMA_OK);
        default:
            fwrite(STDERR, 'loghound-schema: unknown option ' . $arg . "\n");
            lh_schema_usage(STDERR);
            exit(LH_SCHEMA_CONFIG);
    }
}

$cfg = Config::load($configPath);
$errors = $cfg->validate();
if ($errors !== []) {
    fwrite(STDERR, "Loghound: configuration is not usable:\n  - " . implode("\n  - ", $errors) . "\n");
    exit(LH_SCHEMA_CONFIG);
}

$say = static function (string $line) use ($quiet): void {
    if (!$quiet) {
        fwrite(STDOUT, $line . "\n");
    }
};

try {
    $report = Schema::inspect($cfg, $root);
} catch (\Throwable $e) {
    fwrite(STDERR, 'Loghound: ' . $e->getMessage() . "\n");
    exit(LH_SCHEMA_FAILED);
}

$say('Loghound schema check');
$say('  installation : ' . $root);
$say('  release      : ' . $report['release']);
$say('');
lh_schema_table($report, $say);

if ($report['state'] === Schema::UNCONFIGURED) {
    Schema::writeCache($cfg, $report);
    fwrite(STDERR, "\nThis installation has no indexes yet. Run bin/loghound-setup first.\n");
    exit(LH_SCHEMA_CONFIG);
}

if (!$apply) {
    $saved = Schema::writeCache($cfg, $report);
    lh_schema_verdict($report, $root, $saved, false);

    exit(Schema::exitCode((string) $report['state']));
}

/* CURRENT MEANS THE FIELDS MATCH, AND NOTHING MORE. It is not a statement about solrconfig, so
   a release that changes an update chain, a processor or a handler leaves every index reporting
   CURRENT while the change never reaches one. --force is how an operator uploads anyway. */
if ($report['state'] === Schema::CURRENT && !$force) {
    $saved = Schema::writeCache($cfg, $report);
    lh_schema_verdict($report, $root, $saved, false);
    fwrite(STDOUT, "Nothing to apply. The fields already match on both indexes.\n"
        . "That compares FIELDS only — it cannot see a solrconfig change. To upload this\n"
        . "release's configsets anyway: " . Schema::command($root) . " --force\n");
    exit(LH_SCHEMA_OK);
}

$say('');
$say('Applying this release\'s configsets.');

try {
    $result = Schema::apply($cfg, $root, $report, null, $say, $force);
} catch (\Throwable $e) {
    fwrite(STDERR, 'Loghound: the push failed: ' . $e->getMessage() . "\n");
    exit(LH_SCHEMA_FAILED);
}

$say('');
foreach ($result['indexes'] as $row) {
    $line = '  ' . str_pad((string) $row['role'], 10) . strtoupper((string) $row['state']) . "\n"
        . '      ' . wordwrap((string) $row['message'], 84, "\n      ", true);
    if ($row['state'] === 'failed' || $row['state'] === 'refused') {
        fwrite(STDERR, $line . "\n");
        continue;
    }
    $say($line);
}

if ($result['state'] === 'failed') {
    Schema::writeCache($cfg, Schema::inspect($cfg, $root));
    fwrite(STDERR, "\nThe push did not finish. Nothing above is a guess: each line names what happened "
        . "to that index and what state it is in now.\n");
    exit(LH_SCHEMA_FAILED);
}

/**
 * Re-read the live schemas after a push, rather than assuming it landed.
 *
 * The platform accepted the upload and reloaded the core, which is not the same statement as
 * "the core came back up running these fields". This costs one more read per index and it is
 * the difference between reporting what was sent and reporting what is there.
 */
try {
    $after = Schema::inspect($cfg, $root);
} catch (\Throwable $e) {
    fwrite(STDERR, 'Loghound: the configsets were uploaded, but the result could not be re-read: '
        . $e->getMessage() . "\n");
    exit(LH_SCHEMA_FAILED);
}

$say('');
lh_schema_table($after, $say);
$saved = Schema::writeCache($cfg, $after);
lh_schema_verdict($after, $root, $saved, true);

exit(Schema::exitCode((string) $after['state'], true));

/**
 * Print one line per index: role, name, verdict, and what is missing.
 *
 * The lines themselves are Schema::tableLines(), so the command and the panel cannot describe
 * the same state in two different ways; this decides only which stream each one goes to.
 *
 * @param array<string,mixed> $report
 */
function lh_schema_table(array $report, callable $say): void
{
    foreach (Schema::tableLines($report) as $line) {
        if ($line['ok']) {
            $say($line['text']);
            continue;
        }
        fwrite(STDERR, $line['text'] . "\n");
    }
}

/**
 * The closing paragraph: what this means, and the exact command that fixes it.
 *
 * Printed to stderr when something is wrong and stdout when nothing is, so a cron that
 * only mails stderr stays quiet on a healthy installation and is loud on a broken one.
 *
 * @param array<string,mixed> $report
 */
function lh_schema_verdict(array $report, string $root, ?string $saved, bool $applied): void
{
    $verdict = Schema::verdictText($report, $root, $saved, $applied);
    fwrite($verdict['ok'] ? STDOUT : STDERR, $verdict['text']);
}

/** The help text, on stdout for --help and on stderr for a bad flag. */
function lh_schema_usage($stream = STDOUT): void
{
    fwrite($stream, <<<'TEXT'
loghound-schema — check, and fix, the schema your live indexes are running.

  loghound-schema                compare this release against both live schemas, change nothing
  loghound-schema --check        the same, spelled out
  loghound-schema --apply        upload this release's configsets where fields are missing
  loghound-schema --force        upload them even where the fields already match, and reload
  loghound-schema --quiet        print only what is wrong
  loghound-schema --help         this

--apply looks at FIELDS. It uploads to an index that is missing one, and skips an index whose
fields already agree. That is the right default and it has one blind spot: solrconfig.xml. An
update chain, a processor, a request handler or an updateLog can change without a single field
changing, and every index will report "up to date" while the new configset never leaves this
checkout. --force is for exactly that case: it uploads the whole configset to both indexes
whatever the field comparison says, and the platform reloads each core behind it.

--force does NOT override the refusal to push to an index whose live schema could not be read.
That refusal exists so a push never overwrites a schema nobody has looked at, and forcing does
not make that safe.

Exit codes:
  0  up to date
  1  unusable configuration, unknown flag, or no indexes provisioned yet
  2  failed: a schema could not be read, or an upload was rejected
  3  out of date: an index is missing a field this release writes (--check only)
  4  needs migrating: an index still declares Solr's MANAGED schema factory, so Solr owns its
     schema file and every schema upload to it succeeds while changing nothing. --apply fixes
     it by uploading the configset that switches the index to the classic factory.

Run it after every upgrade. A field this release writes that the live schema does not declare
is accepted by Solr and silently discarded, with no error anywhere.

The verdict is written to var/schema-check.json, which is what the panel's Settings page shows.
Set LOGHOUND_CONFIG to run against a configuration file somewhere other than config/.

TEXT);
}
